- Story & Context: Legend says this song was finished during a violent winter gale that snapped the harbor's main mooring lines and sent three unmanned barges drifting into the mist. The band refused to stop recording, turning the screech of metal hulls and the clatter of loose clockwork into a massive, triumphant final movement where the viola flies into an unhinged, sky-high glissando. "The fog-horn knows our names better than the shore," breathlessly hums the vocal ensemble over a crushing, double-speed escapement pulse that suddenly cuts to total silence. The aftermath of the track left the studio gear permanently magnetised, rendering it unable to play conventional digital audio ever again.
